course: english
lesson: 22
title: Reading a story: characters, plot and setting
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every story has characters (who), a setting (where and when) and a plot (what happens). Read this: «Nell found the stray kitten behind the bins. It was thin and wet and hissed when she reached out. She went inside and came back with a saucer of milk and an old towel.» Nobody says Nell is kind and patient, but her actions show it: she helps even after the kitten hisses.
explain: Most stories climb a story mountain: an opening, a build-up, a problem, a resolution where the problem is sorted, and an ending. In Hansel and Gretel, an old fairy tale collected by the Brothers Grimm, Hansel drops breadcrumbs to find the way home, but the birds eat them. The plan failed because he didn't think of the birds: one event causes the next.
explain: Stories carry big ideas. Tim wants to win the race, but stops to help a friend who falls; he comes last and smiles. The message: kindness matters more than winning. In Aesop's old fable, a fox can't reach some grapes and walks off saying, «They're sour anyway.» It teaches that people sometimes pretend they never wanted what they couldn't get.
explain: Small marks can carry big meaning. «Ravi pretended he wasn't scared of the dark. But every night he left the hall light on "by accident".» The quotation marks tell you it wasn't an accident at all. Kirsty in Glasgow says the wee (little) boy in her story is «a right numpty» (a silly person) for losing his shoes; Josh says the main character has «mad rizz» (lots of charm). Both are judging characters, and that's what good readers do.
fact: Characters are the people or animals in a story; we learn what they are like from what they do, say and think.
fact: The setting is where and when a story happens.
fact: The plot is the chain of events in a story.
fact: In many good stories a character changes by the end, showing what they learned from the problem.
fact: A story mountain shows the usual shape: opening, build-up, problem, resolution and ending.
fact: In a plot, one event often causes the next; this is cause and effect.
fact: The theme or message is the big idea of a story, like «kindness matters more than winning».
fact: A fable is a short story, often with animals, that teaches a lesson called a moral.
fact: Aesop's fables are very old stories, told for over two thousand years.
fact: Quotation marks around a word can show that it isn't meant literally, like «by accident» when it was on purpose.
fact: A summary tells the main events in a few words, not every detail.
fact: Your opinion of a story is fine as long as you give a reason.
fact: Slang: «wee» (Scotland) means little; «numpty» (Scotland) means a silly person; «rizz» means charm; «mid» means average.
exercise: Nell helps the kitten even after it hisses. What kind of person is she? | Kind and patient | Scared of all animals and keen to stay as far from them as possible | Bossy | Lazy
exercise: Ben is asked, «Where is the story set?» He says, «In a book.» What does setting mean? | Where and when the story happens | The book itself, with its cover, spine and pages | The person who wrote it | The last line
exercise: Tim helps a friend who falls, comes last, but smiles. What is the message? | Kindness matters more than winning | Winning is the only thing that matters in life, so never stop for anyone | Running is bad for you | Friends always fall over
exercise: Kirsty calls a character «a right numpty». What does numpty mean? | A silly person | A wee dog that yaps | A clever person | A grumpy old man who lives next door
exercise: What are the parts of a story mountain? | Opening, build-up, problem, resolution, ending | Title, cover, page numbers and the price on the back | Start and finish | Problem, problem, problem
exercise: In Aesop's fable, the fox says the grapes he can't reach are sour. What does the fable teach? | People pretend they didn't want what they couldn't get | Grapes are bad for foxes | Foxes are good climbers | Always eat fruit
exercise: Why did Hansel's breadcrumb plan fail? | The birds ate the breadcrumbs | The bread was too stale for anyone to see it on the ground in the dark | He dropped too few | Gretel picked them up
exercise: Josh says the main character «has got mad rizz, everyone likes him». What is he saying? | The character is very charming | The character is very angry about everything that happens to him | The character is mad | The character is rich
exercise: «Every night he left the hall light on "by accident".» What do the quotation marks show? | It wasn't really an accident | Somebody else said those words out loud | The light was broken | It was a very big accident
exercise: «Summarise the story in one sentence.» What does a summary give? | Just the main events | Every single detail, in the same order as the story | The ending and nothing else | Your favourite word in it
exercise: Leo says the ending was «mid». What should he add to make it a good opinion? | A reason why he thinks so | Nothing, «mid» says it all | Ten more slang words to sound cool | The page number of the ending
exercise: Where is the kitten story set? | Behind the bins, by a house | In a deep forest | At a busy railway station in the middle of a big city | In a school
exercise: What is a fable? | A short story that teaches a lesson | A very long story about real people in history | A type of poem | A newspaper report
exercise: What is the plot of a story? | What happens | Who wrote the story | Where the story is set, and at what time of year | The pictures in the book
exercise: How do we usually learn what a character is like? | From what they do, say and think | From the colour of the cover | From the price of the book in the shop | From the title only
test: «Nell's just bored, that's why she got the towel». Which clue shows she is kind, not bored? | She helps the kitten even after it hisses at her | She lives near some bins | She went inside the house | She has an old towel in a cupboard
test: ★★ Cause and effect: Hansel drops breadcrumbs; birds eat them. What is the effect? | The children can't find the way home | The birds get sick and fly away | Hansel gets more bread | The witch helps them home
test: ★★ A girl in a story says she «didn't even want» the part in the play, after she didn't get it. Which story is like this? | The fox and the sour grapes | Hansel and Gretel and the breadcrumbs | The race where Tim helped his friend | The kitten behind the bins
test: «the setting is the bit where she sets the table lol». What's right? | The setting is where and when the story happens | Yes, setting means setting the table | The setting is the last chapter | The setting is the title
test: ★★★ «Ravi pretended he wasn't scared of the dark. But every night he left the hall light on "by accident".» Which is the best summary of Ravi? | He is scared of the dark but doesn't want to admit it | He is not scared and keeps forgetting to switch off the light in the hall | He loves electricity | He wants to annoy his family on purpose
test: ★★★ A summary of Tim's race: which is best? | Tim gives up winning to help a fallen friend, and is happy | Tim runs in a race | Tim has trainers, there is a race, a friend falls, Tim stops, he helps, he runs again, he comes last, he smiles, he goes home | Tim wins the race easily
test: In a cartoon, a hero is good at everything and never changes. Why do many good stories let characters change? | It shows they learned something from the problem | Changing characters is against the rules | Readers hate characters who change | It makes the book shorter
test: What would happen to a story with no problem in the middle? | Not much would happen, so it might be dull | It would be more exciting than any other kind of story | It couldn't have characters | It would have no setting
test: Mum: «Did you like the ending?» Leo: «Nah, it was mid.» Mum: «Why?» Leo: «Dunno.» What's missing from Leo's opinion? | A reason | A spelling | A better title | More slang
test: «the wee boy's a right numpty, he keeps losing his shoes». Why might an author make a character keep making the same mistake? | For humour, or to show him change later | Because the author forgot what happened earlier | To make the book shorter | To teach readers to lose shoes
exam: Identify the principal trait demonstrated by a character who assists a hostile, hissing kitten. | Kindness and patience | Indifference | Cowardice | Greed, in that she hoped to sell the animal for a sum of money
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the setting of a narrative? | The place and time in which the events occur | The sequence of events | The author's name | The moral, or lesson, which the reader is intended to draw from the narrative
exam: Ascertain the moral conveyed by the fable in which a fox, unable to reach certain grapes, declares them to be sour. | That persons may disparage what they are unable to obtain | That grapes are injurious to foxes | That foxes are unable to climb | That one ought always to consume fruit at every available opportunity
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the failure of the breadcrumb trail in the tale of Hansel and Gretel. | The breadcrumbs were consumed by birds | The breadcrumbs were collected by Gretel | The breadcrumbs dissolved in heavy rain that fell throughout the night | The trail was too short
